<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>

    <PackageId>TheKrystalShip.KGSM.Lib</PackageId>
    <Authors>Heisenberg</Authors>
    <Version>1.14.0</Version>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>1.14.0 — Firewall enforcement state (consumes Firewall.Contracts 1.1.0). Surfaces the authority's new enforcement axis so a consumer never reads an inactive firewall as "closed": FirewallListResult gains Enforcement (new FirewallEnforcement enum: Enforcing | Inactive | Unknown) — when Inactive the backend filters NOTHING, so every port is reachable regardless of owned rules; FirewallService maps it from the wire (a pre-1.1.0 authority omits it → Unknown, and the consumer falls back to prior behaviour). FirewallOutcome gains AppliedInactive ("applied-inactive") — a rule written/staged while the backend is inactive (a success: persists and enforces on the operator's next `ufw enable`, and the port is open meanwhile), distinct from an enforced Applied. Additive and non-breaking: the new enum members are appended (existing ordinals unchanged), Enforcement defaults to Unknown, existing call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (these are code-mapped result types, not wire-deserialized — no KgsmJsonContext change). — 1.13.0 — BREAKING (de-ufw rename): IFileService.CreateUfw / RemoveUfw are renamed CreateFirewall / RemoveFirewall, matching the kgsm CLI cutover from the `files ufw enable|disable` verb to `files firewall enable|disable` (kgsm-firewall Inc 4 — the engine no longer renders ufw rules itself; the kgsm-firewall authority owns the host firewall and chooses the backend). The command strings these issue change in lockstep ("files ufw enable" → "files firewall enable", same for disable). Behaviour is otherwise identical — same KgsmResult, same exec path; only the method names + emitted verb changed. The "ufw" in PortMapping.ToUfwSpec and the firewall-backend token ("ufw"/"firewalld"/"none") are NOT affected — those name the stored port-spec format and the detected backend, not the renamed integration verb. Why a rename not an alias: the ecosystem does hard cutovers (no dead aliases), consistent with the 1.10.0 port-format and 1.3.0 systemd removals. — 1.12.0 — Firewall audit event vocabulary (instance_ports_opened / instance_ports_closed). Adds the two event data types kgsm now raises when it opens/closes an instance's host-firewall ports via the kgsm-firewall authority: InstancePortsOpenedData and InstancePortsClosedData, each carrying Ports as the canonical range-preserving List&lt;PortMapping&gt; ([{start,end,protocol}], the same shape `instances info --json` emits — never an opaque UFW string). Both are registered in KgsmJsonContext and EventService's type map, so the C# receive path decodes them (and kgsm-api can emit them via EmitWithProvenance(actor, origin=api) when it consumes this version). VOCABULARY ONLY — FirewallService still performs the firewall op and emits NOTHING; emission stays the caller's responsibility (kgsm bash command layer now; kgsm-api M6 on the C# side), kept out of the transport client so a missing event service can never silently drop the audit trail. Additive and non-breaking: the new types deserialize only when emitted, existing call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (registered types + the already-registered List&lt;PortMapping&gt;). — 1.11.0 — Typed kgsm-firewall control client (IFirewallService / FirewallService): EnsureOpen / Remove / ListOwned / Backend over the host-firewall authority's newline-delimited-JSON unix socket (the authority is NOT HTTP, unlike the watchdog — so this speaks its line protocol directly, not SocketsHttpHandler). Consumes the new shared wire-contract package TheKrystalShip.KGSM.Firewall.Contracts (1.0.0) and maps the canonical PortMapping ↔ the wire PortDto at the boundary, so the ecosystem keeps its single port type; the dependency is one-directional (kgsm-lib → Firewall.Contracts, never the reverse — kgsm-firewall must not reference kgsm-lib, which would be a cycle). Result types preserve the authority's PRECISE outcome rather than a bare bool — FirewallActionResult.Outcome (applied/removed/noop/unsupported/failed) and FirewallListResult.Status with honest Unknown (the backend cannot answer is NOT "nothing open") — the C# analog of the authority's exit-code contract that kgsm-api's future honest "is this port open" verdict depends on. An unreachable authority (no socket / timeout / no reply) throws FirewallException, distinct from a reachable-but-unsuccessful result. Registered via AddKgsmFirewallClient (parallel to AddKgsmWatchdogClient). Reflection-free source-generated JSON (via the Contracts package), Native-AOT/trim-safe. This client performs the firewall op ONLY and emits NO kgsm events — the instance_ports_opened/instance_ports_closed audit events are emitted separately by the caller (kgsm-firewall Inc 3), kept out of the transport client so a missing event service can never silently drop the audit trail. Additive and non-breaking. — 1.10.0 — BREAKING: canonical structured port format. Instance.Ports changes from an opaque UFW-style string ("27015:27020/udp|27016/tcp") to List&lt;PortMapping&gt; — the range-preserving structured form [{start,end,protocol}] KGSM now emits on `instances info --json`. The redundant, pre-expanded Instance.UpnpPorts (bash-array-literal string) is REMOVED (consumers expand the structured form locally), and the unread ConfigurationInfo.Ports (status surface) is REMOVED. New PortMapping record (start/end ints, protocol "tcp"|"udp"; a single port has start==end, a proto-less UFW entry expands to one tcp + one udp mapping) and PortMappingExtensions: Expand() → individual (port, protocol) pairs (what upnpc / a per-port conflict scan need) and ToUfwSpec() → the legacy "start:end/proto|..." string. Registered in KgsmJsonContext (PortMapping + List&lt;PortMapping&gt;); start/end arrive as JSON numbers and bind via the existing global string→int coercion (either wire shape works). This is the single port type kgsm-firewall's EnsureOpen(instance, ports[]) and the watchdog's UPnP path consume — no consumer re-parses a port string. Why breaking-not-additive: a hard cutover (per the headless-network plan) so no dead string field or duplicate parser is left behind. — 1.9.0 — Watchdog crash/give-up events + provenance-stamped emit. Adds two event data types the resident supervisor (kgsm-watchdog) raises for autonomous lifecycle transitions the engine could not previously surface: InstanceCrashedData (instance_crashed — a desired-running process died unexpectedly and is being auto-restarted; warn) and InstanceFailedData (instance_failed — the supervisor exhausted its restart retries and gave up; the escalation signal). Both carry ExitCode (string; the literal "unknown" when unreadable — never a fabricated code) and Restarts (string; the consecutive-failure count), are registered in KgsmJsonContext + EventService's type map, and are stamped Actor="system"/Origin="system" (autonomous engine action, not a human surface). To let the watchdog stamp that provenance, IEventManagementService gains EmitWithProvenance(eventType, actor, origin, params) — a distinct method (not an Emit overload, to avoid resolution ambiguity with the existing params-only Emit) that layers $KGSM_EVENT_ACTOR/$KGSM_EVENT_ORIGIN onto the one kgsm invocation via the 1.8.0 IKgsmCommandExecutor environment overload, mirroring LifecycleService's provenance plumbing. Additive and non-breaking: new types deserialize only when emitted, existing Emit call sites compile unchanged, AOT-safe (registered types + plain env vars). — 1.8.0 — Event origin + caller-supplied lifecycle provenance. Completes the audit-provenance pair started in 1.6.0: the event envelope (EventWrapper) and EventDataBase now also surface Origin (string?) — the surface that drove the event (ui|assistant|discord|system|api), the companion to Actor (who) answering "through which surface". EventService copies it from the envelope onto each dispatched event-data object, like Actor/Timestamp. KGSM takes it from $KGSM_EVENT_ORIGIN; unlike the actor there is NO fallback for a bare CLI call (no product surface), so an absent origin stays null — never fabricated. To let a caller actually stamp this, ILifecycleService.Start/Stop/Restart gain optional (actor, origin) parameters that are propagated to KGSM as $KGSM_EVENT_ACTOR/$KGSM_EVENT_ORIGIN for that one invocation (new IProcessRunner/IKgsmCommandExecutor environment overloads layer them onto the child process, never mutating the lib's own environment); omitted values fall back to KGSM's own honest defaults. Additive and non-breaking: the new params default to null, a pre-origin payload deserializes Origin as null, and existing call sites compile unchanged. AOT-safe (Origin is a property of the already-registered EventWrapper; provenance is plain env vars). — 1.7.0 — Unified ecosystem health probe. WatchdogClient now probes the watchdog's GET /health (was /ready) for both IsReadyAsync and GetReadyAsync; the daemon serves /health as the canonical readiness path (200 ready / 503 + reason) and keeps /ready as a deprecated alias for one transition release, so a consumer still on 1.6.0 keeps working unchanged. Response shape is unchanged ({ready,detail} → WatchdogReadyState) and no method signature changed — additive and non-breaking. Part of standardizing every ecosystem leaf on a single GET /health. — 1.6.0 — Event audit enrichment (who/when). The event envelope (EventWrapper) now surfaces the top-level metadata KGSM already emits but the lib previously dropped: Timestamp (DateTimeOffset?), Actor (string?), Hostname (string?), and KgsmVersion (string?, bound from the wire's KGSMVersion). EventDataBase gains Timestamp + Actor, and EventService copies them from the envelope onto each dispatched event-data object — so handlers see who triggered an event and when, with no handler-signature change. Actor is the audit principal: KGSM takes it from $KGSM_EVENT_ACTOR (set by the calling surface — bot/assistant/watchdog) or falls back to the invoking OS user; null/absent is preserved as null, never a fabricated identity or time. Additive and non-breaking: a pre-enrichment payload leaves the new fields null. This is the consumer half of the event-enrichment work (kgsm emits, lib surfaces); event *persistence* lives downstream of the stateless engine. — 1.5.0 — Native cgroup path, plus the first package to ship three already-committed post-1.4.0 changes. NEW: Instance.CgroupPath — the per-instance cgroup v2 directory KGSM derives for a native instance (kgsm.slice/&lt;name&gt;), the exact path kgsm-watchdog (re)creates on every native start; empty for container instances (Docker owns their cgroup) and when KGSM omits the field. Lets kgsm-monitor read native cgroup counters directly (CPU/mem/pids/io) instead of the /proc process-tree fallback, without re-deriving the cgroup layout or opening the watchdog socket — additive, JSON without cgroup_path deserializes CgroupPath as empty. ALSO FIRST PACKAGED HERE (committed on main after the 1.4.0 cut): (1) Reading&lt;T&gt; typed-unknown envelope — **BREAKING**: GetAllStatuses now returns Dictionary&lt;string, Reading&lt;InstanceRuntimeStatus&gt;&gt; (was Dictionary&lt;string, InstanceRuntimeStatus&gt;); (2) InstanceService.GetInstanceConfigValue/SetInstanceConfigValue — read/write a single key in an instance .config.ini; (3) typed host SystemInfo model + ISystemService.GetSystemInfo(). — 1.4.0 — Blueprint game metadata. Blueprint gains a nullable Metadata object (BlueprintMetadata: DisplayName, Description, MaxPlayers, MinRamMb, RecommendedRamMb, BaseDiskMb) mirroring the new nested "Metadata" block emitted by `kgsm blueprints info --json`. All values are nullable: an unknown value is null, never a fabricated 0. Additive and non-breaking — JSON from older KGSM without the block deserializes Metadata as null. (KGSM itself moved to a unified per-game .bp.yaml blueprint format, but the lib only ever consumes the JSON wire shape, which is unchanged except for the added Metadata object.) — 1.3.0 — BREAKING: systemd removed as a KGSM instance lifecycle manager. Deleted the LifecycleManager enum and Instance.LifecycleManager/EnableSystemd/SystemdServiceFile/SystemdSocketFile/SystemdUnit, ConfigurationInfo.LifecycleManager, and the LifecycleManager field on the instance-started/stopped/restarted event payloads. Instance.Runtime (native|container) is now the sole supervision discriminator. Legacy lifecycle_manager/systemd_* JSON fields older KGSM still emits are tolerated (unmapped, ignored). — 1.2.0: Typed kgsm-watchdog control client (IWatchdogClient / WatchdogClient): start/stop/status/list/ready over the daemon's unix socket (HTTP/1.1 via SocketsHttpHandler.ConnectCallback), reflection-free source-generated JSON (Native AOT/trim-safe), registered via AddKgsmWatchdogClient. — 1.1.0: Bulk fleet status read (GetAllStatuses, with --fast); restart and *-failed event types; reflection-free source-generated JSON for Native AOT/trim safety.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/TheKrystalShip/KGSM-Lib</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/TheKrystalShip/KGSM-Lib</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>GitHub</RepositoryType>
    <Description>Classlibrary for KGSM Interop</Description>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>GPL-3.0-only</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <NeutralLanguage>en</NeutralLanguage>
    <GeneratePackageOnBuild>true</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
    <RootNamespace>TheKrystalShip.KGSM</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>TheKrystalShip.KGSM</AssemblyName>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <DocumentationFile>bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\$(AssemblyName).xml</DocumentationFile>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
      <None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="9.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="9.0.0" />
    <!-- The kgsm-firewall control-socket wire contract (DTOs + newline-JSON framing + source-gen
         context) that IFirewallService speaks. Resolved from the local-nuget folder feed (see
         ../nuget.config). One-directional: kgsm-lib depends on this, never the reverse — kgsm-firewall
         must not reference kgsm-lib (cycle). net9.0 + AOT/trim-clean, so it keeps kgsm-lib AOT-safe. -->
    <PackageReference Include="TheKrystalShip.KGSM.Firewall.Contracts" Version="1.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
